 "chunkId": "work-school-2",
 "label": "Questions and learning",
 "topic": "Work & school",
 "rationale": "Class talk: having a question, answering one, speaking English, then the thanks phrase benkyou ni naru.",
 "mixUps": "shitsumon ga aru (have one) takes ga and shitsumon ni kotaeru (answer it) takes ni, never o; benkyou ni natta (I learned from it) is not benkyou shita (I studied).",
 "targets": [
  {
   "id": "c-shitsumon-ga-aru",
   "romaji": "shitsumon ga aru",
   "polite": "shitsumon ga arimasu",
   "english": "have a question",
   "ja": "質問がある",
   "kana": "しつもんがある",
   "politeJa": "質問があります",
   "noun": "shitsumon",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "aru",
   "verbJa": "ある",
   "verbSense": "there is; have (things, plans, something to bring up)",
   "englishVerb": "have",
   "trap": "English 'have' leads to motsu, but a question you have is aru: shitsumon ga aru. Asking it is shitsumon suru, or simply kiku.",
   "partners": [
    "soudan (something to talk over)",
    "onegai (favour)",
    "hanashi (something to talk about)",
    "iken (opinion, objection)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Said to open a question, usually with n da kedo, and here the ga mostly stays: shitsumon ga aru n da kedo. After nanika it's often dropped or becomes wa: teachers and presenters ask the room nanika shitsumon (wa) arimasu ka?",
   "notes": "To check before asking: shitsumon ii? (can I ask something?). Answering one is shitsumon ni kotaeru.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "hitotsu shitsumon ga aru n da kedo.",
    "ja": "一つ質問があるんだけど。",
    "english": "I've got a question for you."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "hitotsu shitsumon ga aru n desu kedo.",
    "ja": "一つ質問があるんですけど。",
    "english": "I've got a question for you."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-shitsumon-ni-kotaeru",
   "romaji": "shitsumon ni kotaeru",
   "polite": "shitsumon ni kotaemasu",
   "english": "answer a question",
   "ja": "質問に答える",
   "kana": "しつもんにこたえる",
   "politeJa": "質問に答えます",
   "noun": "shitsumon",
   "particle": "ni",
   "verb": "kotaeru",
   "verbJa": "答える",
   "verbSense": "answer, reply",
   "englishVerb": "answer",
   "trap": "English 'answer the question' has a direct object, which tempts shitsumon o kotaeru. In Japanese you answer to a question: shitsumon ni kotaeru. Answering the phone is a different verb: denwa ni deru.",
   "partners": [
    "ankeeto (survey)",
    "intabyuu (interview)",
    "toi (question, in writing)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Said in class, interviews and arguments, often as a demand (chanto shitsumon ni kotaete) or a refusal (sono shitsumon ni wa kotaerarenai). The ni almost always stays.",
   "notes": "Asking is shitsumon suru or just kiku; the answer itself is kotae (kotae wa?).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "sono shitsumon ni wa kotaerarenai.",
    "ja": "その質問には答えられない。",
    "english": "I can't answer that question."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "sono shitsumon ni wa kotaeraremasen.",
    "ja": "その質問には答えられません。",
    "english": "I can't answer that question."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-eigo-o-hanasu",
   "romaji": "eigo o hanasu",
   "polite": "eigo o hanashimasu",
   "english": "speak English",
   "ja": "英語を話す",
   "kana": "えいごをはなす",
   "politeJa": "英語を話します",
   "noun": "eigo",
   "particle": "o",
   "verb": "hanasu",
   "verbJa": "話す",
   "verbSense": "speak, talk; tell",
   "englishVerb": "speak",
   "trap": "'Do you speak English?' is a plain present in English, but asking whether someone can takes the potential: eigo hanaseru? or eigo dekiru? Plain eigo hanasu? is understood, but it asks whether someone does speak it somewhere (ie de eigo hanasu no? = do you speak English at home?) or is about to, not whether they can.",
   "partners": [
    "nihongo (Japanese)",
    "kankokugo (Korean)",
    "chuugokugo (Chinese)",
    "kansaiben (Kansai dialect)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Mostly heard about ability, in the potential: eigo hanaseru? (can you speak English?). With the potential, ga is as common as o: eigo ga hanaseru. Casual speech drops o. eigo de hanasu means talking in English at that moment: eigo de hanashite mo ii?",
   "notes": "eigo dekiru? asks the same thing. Whether English gets you by somewhere is tsuujiru: eigo tsuujiru?",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "eigo wa chotto dake hanaseru.",
    "ja": "英語はちょっとだけ話せる。",
    "english": "I can speak a little English."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "eigo wa chotto dake hanasemasu.",
    "ja": "英語はちょっとだけ話せます。",
    "english": "I can speak a little English."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-benkyou-ni-naru",
   "romaji": "benkyou ni naru",
   "polite": "benkyou ni narimasu",
   "english": "be instructive; learn a lot",
   "ja": "勉強になる",
   "kana": "べんきょうになる",
   "politeJa": "勉強になります",
   "noun": "benkyou",
   "particle": "ni",
   "verb": "naru",
   "verbJa": "なる",
   "verbSense": "become, turn into",
   "englishVerb": "learn",
   "trap": "benkyou shita means you sat down and studied. 'I learned a lot from that' is benkyou ni natta: the thing becomes a lesson.",
   "partners": [
    "sankou (useful reference)",
    "tame (good for you)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Said as thanks when someone explains or shows you something: benkyou ni narimasu as a reply on the spot, especially to a senior, and benkyou ni natta or benkyou ni narimashita afterwards.",
   "notes": "Often comes after naruhodo: naruhodo, benkyou ni narimasu.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "kyou no hanashi, sugoku benkyou ni natta.",
    "ja": "今日の話、すごく勉強になった。",
    "english": "I learned a lot from today's talk."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "kyou no ohanashi, hontou ni benkyou ni narimashita.",
    "ja": "今日のお話、本当に勉強になりました。",
    "english": "I learned a lot from today's talk."
   }
  }
 ],
